     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the labeled maximum daily dose of 2,550 mg for immediate-release metformin. For extended-release the maximum is 2,000 mg/day. A 1,700 mg single dose is also well above the usual maximum per-dose amount of 850 mg to 1,000 mg. This much metformin raises the risk of serious GI toxicity and lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nHere are some options that stay within the label:\n- **Immediate-release:** up to 850 mg three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or 1,000 mg twice daily (2,000 mg/day).\n- **Extended-release:** up to 2,000 mg once daily with the evening meal.\n- **Titration:** start at 500 mg twice daily, or 850 mg once daily, and increase gradually as tolerated.\n\nThe order may have a typo. For example, you may have meant 850 mg or 500 mg three times daily, or 1,700 mg total per day. Tell me the dose you want and I'll enter it. If you need more glycemic control than the maximum dose gives, adding another agent is usually the next step. That decision is for the prescriber and pharmacy."
      }
     ],
